          "text": "Leo, strongest correction: **UUIDs do not prevent conflicts.** They prevent identity collisions. The conflict-free property comes from:\n\n`UUID identity + immutable records + append-only operations + explicit parentage`\n\nConfidence: high.\n\n| State | Git/UUID result | Remaining conflict |\n|---|---|---|\n| Chat messages | Concurrent UUID-addressed additions merge cleanly | Two children of one parent become an explicit conversation fork |\n| Message edits | Clean if represented as new `supersedes` events | In-place editing can conflict |\n| Chat metadata | Clean if represented as events | Shared mutable `chat.json` will conflict |\n| KB articles | Isolated changes, reviewable merge | Two Runs can semantically edit the same article |\n| Run execution | Essentially no help | Authority, approvals, and side effects still require fencing |\n| Deletion | Tombstone can merge | Physical erasure from every clone remains separate |\n\nGit itself still reports ordinary conflicts when divergent commits modify overlapping content. jj can preserve those conflicts as first-class objects, but it cannot decide which meaning is correct. [Git merge documentation](https://git-scm.com/docs/git-merge.html), [jj conflict documentation](https://jj-vcs.github.io/jj/latest/conflicts/).\n\nThe important architectural refinement: isolation should normally be **per Run branch**, not permanently per Chat.\n\n```text\nChat UUID\n└── Run-branch UUID\n    ├── exact input KB revision\n    ├── unique Git branch / jj working-copy commit\n    ├── isolated worktree\n    └── candidate commits proposed to the KB\n```\n\nA Chat is a durable conversation DAG. It may fork, resume on another node, or contain several sequential Runs. Binding one permanent worktree to it conflates conversation identity with temporary execution state. If by “session” you mean the active Run branch, then your model is sound.\n\nAlso, a Git worktree is VCS isolation, not filesystem or security isolation. It shares the repository object store and most refs; Git normally prevents checking out the same branch in multiple worktrees. Each concurrent Run therefore needs its own ref. [Git worktree documentation](https://git-scm.com/docs/git-worktree.html).\n\n### Does Git require something central?\n\nNo—not for personal replication.\n\nPeers can exchange commits directly, and any node can merge them. A remote is useful as a durable rendezvous and backup, but it need not be the user’s “home.”\n\nFor shared KBs, however, you still need a **governed accepted ref**:\n\n```text\nPersonal KB → llame hub, direct peer, bare SSH Git, etc.\nFamily KB   → private GitHub or Forgejo repository\nWork KB     → company GitLab\nPublic KB   → public GitHub repository\n```\n\nEach Knowledge Space can have a different upstream and governance authority. The local node mirrors all spaces it may access. There is no single global home.\n\nGitHub is therefore an optional implementation of:\n\n- object transport;\n- canonical accepted branch;\n- authentication and access control;\n- proposal/PR workflow;\n- protected-ref advancement.\n\nThe architecture should depend on Git commits/refs and policy, not GitHub-specific semantics. Ref updates can use compare-and-swap behavior to reject stale writers. [Git `update-ref` documentation](https://git-scm.com/docs/git-update-ref.html).\n\n### What must remain outside Git\n\nDo not put these into merge-driven state:\n\n- active Run authority and authority epochs;\n- worker leases and queues;\n- pending permission prompts;\n- tool-call settlement and external side effects;\n- node enrollment and cryptographic revocation;\n- secrets;\n- live stream cursors and temporary availability.\n\nThose are control-plane facts requiring immediate, fenced decisions. Git’s eventual convergence cannot guarantee that only one executor sends an email, modifies a repository, or consumes an approval.\n\nMy recommendation:\n\n1. **Git content plane:** KB revisions, immutable finalized chat events, summaries, candidate skills/configuration.\n2. **Operational control plane:** Run authority, queues, permissions, node identity, credentials, live execution.\n3. **SQLite/Postgres projections:** rebuildable indexes over Git content for search and UI performance.\n4. **Per-Run-branch isolation:** reuse it across ordinary continuation messages; fork it when the conversation forks.\n5. **Git transport boundary:** jj may provide superior local UX, but do not sync raw `.jj` directories between machines. jj currently documents hazards around cross-machine concurrent repository synchronization; exchange Git commits and refs instead. [jj concurrency documentation](https://jj-vcs.github.io/jj/latest/technical/concurrency/).\n\nSo yes: this materially simplifies **content federation** and weakens the need for a central personal home. It does not change the need for authority over execution or acceptance into governed shared spaces.\n\nThe next decision to lock is: **finalized chat events become canonical immutable Git content, while live Run state remains operational state.** Confidence in that direction: moderate; it needs a scale prototype before becoming a hard contract.",
