          "text": "Leo, the dangerous conclusion would be “Buzz validates Nostr federation.” It does not. Buzz explicitly has one authoritative relay and “no peer-to-peer exchange, gossip, or replication” [ARCHITECTURE.md](/home/leon0399/.cache/checkouts/github.com/block/buzz/ARCHITECTURE.md:7). Goose is likewise a single-machine agent, not a federation implementation.\n\nCore direction: unchanged — high confidence.\n\n| Area | Conclusion |\n|---|---|\n| Personal Realm | Keep many trusted replicas; neither project solves this. |\n| Shared resources | Keep one governing authority per Space. Buzz actually reinforces this model. |\n| Chats | Keep database-native episodic state synchronized through llame’s protocol. Goose’s SQLite is an embedded engine, not a replication design [session_manager.rs](/home/leon0399/.cache/checkouts/github.com/aaif-goose/goose/crates/goose/src/session/session_manager.rs:980). |\n| Knowledge | Keep Git history plus raw-Git/forge publication adapters. Buzz’s Nostr memory has best-effort enumeration and no authoritative version chain, making it weaker than our design. |\n| Nostr | Retain signed envelopes as a possible provenance mechanism; do not adopt Nostr as llame’s database or identity model. |\n| Run authority | Keep transferable single-executor authority. Buzz and Goose provide useful implementation constraints, not an alternative topology. |\n\nWhat materially improves our design:\n\n1. **Synchronization needs an explicit completeness state — high confidence.**\n\n   Buzz’s read-state protocol distinguishes `complete` from `cannot prove complete` and forbids canonicalization or deletion from an incomplete view [NIP-RS.md](/home/leon0399/.cache/checkouts/github.com/block/buzz/docs/nips/NIP-RS.md:321).\n\n   We should add this invariant:\n\n   > Every replica bootstrap/resume has a verifiable completeness boundary. A partial replica may ingest additive records but cannot compact tombstones, authorize deletion, claim reconciliation complete, or replace accepted state.\n\n   This is the most important new requirement.\n\n2. **Goose’s permission pipeline is useful structurally but unsafe to copy — high confidence.**\n\n   Useful:\n\n   - typed `Allow | RequireApproval | Deny`;\n   - restrictive composition where allow cannot override ask/deny [tool_inspection.rs](/home/leon0399/.cache/checkouts/github.com/aaif-goose/goose/crates/goose/src/tool_inspection.rs:213);\n   - tool requests explicitly framed as untrusted data [permission_judge.rs](/home/leon0399/.cache/checkouts/github.com/aaif-goose/goose/crates/goose/src/permission/permission_judge.rs:65).\n\n   Unacceptable for llame:\n\n   - inspector failures are logged and ignored—per-inspector fail-open [tool_inspection.rs](/home/leon0399/.cache/checkouts/github.com/aaif-goose/goose/crates/goose/src/tool_inspection.rs:95);\n   - the LLM judge can independently turn a request into `Allow`;\n   - active persisted policies are tool-name-wide [permission.rs](/home/leon0399/.cache/checkouts/github.com/aaif-goose/goose/crates/goose/src/config/permission.rs:145), not scoped to `EnterWorkspace(workspace_id)`.\n\n   Our engine should fail closed, canonicalize parameters, and allow model-based inspectors only to escalate `allow → ask/deny`, never expand authority.\n\n3. **Executor handoff has two different artifacts — moderate-high confidence.**\n\n   Goose creates a bounded, redacted context memo when an ACP target cannot resume the original provider session [handoff.rs](/home/leon0399/.cache/checkouts/github.com/aaif-goose/goose/crates/goose/src/acp/handoff.rs:1).\n\n   For llame:\n\n   - canonical Chat/Run records and IDs remain durable synchronization state;\n   - an executor continuation capsule is a derived, bounded projection for one target;\n   - losing or regenerating that capsule must not alter lineage or authority history.\n\n4. **Buzz’s formal conformance style should become an implementation task — high confidence.**\n\n   Its RLS checklist covers restrictive policies, role owne********ORCE RLS, transaction-local tenant identity, `SECURITY DEFINER`/leakproof functions, and tenant-qualified unique/FK constraints [multi-tenant-relay.md](/home/leon0399/.cache/checkouts/github.com/block/buzz/docs/multi-tenant-relay.md:344). It then makes those startup/CI deployment gates [multi-tenant-relay.md](/home/leon0399/.cache/checkouts/github.com/block/buzz/docs/multi-tenant-relay.md:651).\n\n   This does not belong primarily in the federation vision. It deserves a separate llame RLS conformance audit, especially A-RLS-4 and A-RLS-5.\n\n5. **Remote-executor operations need fenced reconciliation — high confidence.**\n\n   Buzz treats provider output as untrusted and redacts it [remote-agents.md](/home/leon0399/.cache/checkouts/github.com/block/buzz/docs/remote-agents.md:378); destructive reconciliation is fenced to the exact observation that authorized it [remote-agents.md](/home/leon0399/.cache/checkouts/github.com/block/buzz/docs/remote-agents.md:1468). That belongs in our daemon/sandbox executor contract.\n\nThe `CLAU********l.md` summaries need corrections:\n\n- Buzz desktop is **Tauri + React**, not Electron.\n- Explicitly label Buzz a centralized-relay reference, not a federation/mirroring reference.\n- Goose is now at `469bea969`; changes since the recorded revision are dependency/workflow-only.\n- “HEAD is a security fix” is now stale.\n- The Goose permission-store description is misleading: the argument-hashed [permission_store.rs](/home/leon0399/.cache/checkouts/github.com/aaif-goose/goose/crates/goose/src/permission/permission_store.rs:11) has no active consumers beyond its definition/re-export. Actual user permissions are name-wide.\n- Worth adding: Goose’s encrypted Nostr session feature is snapshot export/import [nostr_share.rs](/home/leon0399/.cache/checkouts/github.com/aaif-goose/goose/crates/goose/src/session/nostr_share.rs:145), not synchronization.\n\nPrioritized next move:\n\n1. Correct `CLAU********l.md`