become substantially harder; conflicts still require domain rules |\n| Semantic change batches | Preserves normal domain storage while supporting heterogeneous replicas | Requires explicit mutation vocabulary and reconciliation rules |\n\nRecommendation: semantic change batches. **Confidence: high.**\n\n## Precise boundary\n\nThree different objects, not one overloaded “outbox”:\n\n1. **Domain state**  \n   Canonical Chats, messages, compactions, finalized Run data, tombstones, and revisions.\n\n2. **Replication journal**  \n   Immutable, portable `ChangeBatch` objects describing semantic mutations. A batch can contain several related operations and must be applied atomically.\n\n3. **Delivery state**  \n   Per-link outbox, acknowledgements, retry attempts, cursors, and deduplication records. Entirely local and disposable after a safe checkpoint.\n\nEvery portable local mutation commits:\n\n```text\ndomain-state changes + one ChangeBatch\n```\n\nin the same transaction. Receiving a batch commits:\n\n```text\ndeduplication record + reconciled domain-state changes + retained original ChangeBatch\n```\n\nalso atomically. The original batch ID and origin remain unchanged so it can be forwarded without manufacturing echo mutations.\n\nA `ChangeBatch` minimally needs:\n\n- globally stable batch ID;\n- Personal Realm ID;\n- originating node identity—not hub-specific `userId`;\n- schema/protocol version;\n- affected resource IDs;\n- causal dependencies or expected revisions;\n- typed semantic operations;\n- integrity hash/signature metadata.\n\n## Concrete examples\n\nAn accepted turn might produce one batch:\n\n```text\nTurnAccepted\n  ChatCreated? \n  UserMessageAppended\n  ContextSnapshotBound\n  RunRecorded\n```\n\nA completed turn produces another:\n\n```text\nTurnSettled\n  RunCompleted\n  AssistantMessageFinalized\n```\n\nDeletion produces one durable `ChatDeleted` tombstone—not hundreds of replicated cascade deletions.\n\nThe current `run_events` table must not become the replication journal. It is execution-stream history with database-local ordering and includes transient deltas. It may later be replicated as Chat data, but receiving `run.created` must never enqueue or re-execute that Run. Execution authority and handoff remain separate.\n\nBootstrap is likewise separate from incremental delivery:\n\n```text\nconsistent portable snapshot at frontier F\n+ ChangeBatches after F\n```\n\nIf a peer returns after its incremental history was pruned, it performs a new snapshot reconciliation. It must not guess continuity.\n\nThis applies to database-native personal state. Git-backed KBs retain Git commits and forge adapters as their synchronization mechanism; attempting one atomic transaction across Postgres and Git would be fake atomicity.\n\nPrioritized next sequence:\n\n1. Lock this three-layer boundary.\n2. Decide exactly which Chat/Run artifacts belong to portable domain state.\n3. Define reconciliation per resource—messages, metadata, deletion, compactions.\n4. Only then design envelope schemas and transport.\n\nThe contract decision now: **ordinary domain records remain canonical; immutable semantic `ChangeBatch` objects are the portable replication unit; existing database rows and `run_events` are explicitly not the federation protocol. Agree?**",
