odules and capabilities it actually supports.\n\n## Proposed contract\n\n### `core.*` — mandatory protocol kernel\n\nShared by every implementation:\n\n- Protocol and module-version negotiation.\n- Authenticated principal and connection scopes.\n- Node identity, role, and capabilities.\n- Request IDs, deadlines, cancellation, and structured errors.\n- Pagination and subscription conventions.\n- Health and connection-generation information.\n\nIllustrative handshake:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"method\": \"core.hello\",\n  \"params\": {\n    \"protocol_version\": \"1\",\n    \"supported_modules\": {\n      \"realm\": [\"1\"],\n      \"execution\": [\"1\"]\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nResponse:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"node_id\": \"node_...\",\n  \"roles\": [\"personal\", \"executor\"],\n  \"modules\": {\n    \"realm\": \"1\",\n    \"execution\": \"1\",\n    \"sync\": \"1\",\n    \"admin\": \"1\"\n  },\n  \"capabilities\": {\n    \"workspace\": true,\n    \"shell\": true,\n    \"local_inference\": false\n  }\n}\n```\n\nIdentity must come from the authenticated channel. Methods do not accept a caller-selected `user_id`. A hosted implementation maps the session to its tenant and RLS scope; a single-user node maps it to its sole local owner.\n\n### `realm.*` — durable user-facing domain state\n\nOwns portable personal state:\n\n- Chats, branches, messages, and finalized Runs.\n- Knowledge Space catalogue and records.\n- Agent Profiles, memories, and user settings.\n- Mounts and Authority Connections.\n- Durable placement records and recovery preferences.\n- Deletion and tombstone operations.\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nrealm.chats.create\nrealm.messages.append\nrealm.chats.fork\nrealm.knowledge.list\nrealm.knowledge.read\nrealm.settings.update\n```\n\nPersonal Realm operations may work offline and synchronize later. Their write contract therefore needs stable IDs, causal parents or expected revisions, and explicit conflict/fork results.\n\nIt exposes domain operations—not SQL, table rows, or generic database replication.\n\n### `execution.*` — active single-authority execution\n\nOwns live executor behavior:\n\n- Executor and sandbox capabilities.\n- Workspace discovery and entry.\n- Placement and Run startup.\n- Current Run snapshots.\n- Live subscription.\n- Approval responses, steering, cancellation, and follow-ups.\n- Command disposition after an ambiguous disconnect.\n\nIllustrative flow:\n\n```text\nexecution.runs.attach(run_id)\n  → snapshot at revision 82\n\nexecution.runs.subscribe(run_id, after_revision=82)\n  → live deltas\n\nexecution.commands.submit(\n  command_id,\n  run_id,\n  authority_epoch,\n  permission_response\n)\n```\n\nWrites require:\n\n- Stable command ID.\n- Exact Run/session target.\n- Current authority epoch.\n- Executor-side authorization and deduplication.\n- Explicit `not_accepted`, `accepted`, `completed`, or `outcome_unknown` results.\n\nUnlike `realm.*`, execution commands are not accepted while the authoritative executor is offline.\n\n### `sync.*` — replica-to-replica Personal Realm synchronization\n\nNot a UI CRUD API. It carries:\n\n- Portable snapshots and frontiers.\n- Semantic `ChangeBatch` records.\n- Coverage proofs.\n- Incremental pull/push.\n- Deduplication and acknowledgement.\n- Schema compatibility.\n- Tombstone and retention mechanics.\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nsync.snapshot.describe\nsync.changes.pull\nsync.changes.push\nsync.frontier.confirm\n```\n\nA linked personal daemon uses this in the background. A browser normally does not receive this capability.\n\nSeparating it from `realm.*` prevents public application operations from becoming accidental replication primitives.\n\n### `admin.*` — privileged node-local management\n\nOwns:\n\n- Link, unlink, enrollment, and revocation.\n- Workspace registration.\n- Provider and model configuration.\n- Sandbox-definition management.\n- Node diagnostics, backup, and maintenance.\n- Secret creation or replacement.\n\nThis module is local-only or strong-auth-only by def