ce-means-no-same-branch-failover rule are settled. Specify the placement,\\n-   checkpoint, barrier, prepare, compare-and-swap, acknowledgement, retry,\\n-   cancellation, and command-disposition schemas plus process-kill conformance\\n-   cases. Keep peer-hosted or movable fencing as later follow-ups until their\\n-   promotion trigger exists.\\n-2. **Enrollment and device-loss mechanics.** Disposable node identity,\\n-   OAuth-authorized enrollment, permanent cryptographic revocation, new-identity\\n-   relinking, and the limits of remote revocation are settled for the initial\\n-   contract. Specify platform key storage, enrollment confirmation, credential\\n-   renewal, revocation delivery, audit, and negative conformance cases. Keep\\n-   identity-preserving rotation, encrypted recovery, peer-distributed revocation,\\n-   hub-loss re-homing, and offline-authorship cutoffs as north-star follow-ups.\\n-3. **Personal Realm operational replication.** The authority map and storage-class\\n-   boundary are settled: trusted Personal Realm replicas may originate personal\\n-   state; Chats are database-native; every shared Space retains one governing\\n-   authority; every Chat or Run branch has a destination authority; and active\\n-   Run execution remains single-authority. Coverage must be explicit and cannot be\\n-   inferred from conflict absence. The semantic `ChangeBatch`, replication-journal,\\n-   and per-link delivery-state split is settled. Specify exact portable resource\\n-   schemas, authoritative snapshot and frontier mechanics, journal retention,\\n-   cursors, causal ordering, resumability, schema evolution, and per-resource\\n-   authorship plus merge/fork/proposal/rejection behavior. The capability-scoped\\n-   authorship north star, unified first-and-later reconciliation function,\\n-   snapshot-as-journal-prefix rule, three-layer active-Run durability boundary,\\n-   and the first-delivery resumable episodic record classes are settled; the\\n-   exact linked writer policy, causal ordering and fork behavior, schemas,\\n-   retention, artifact payload transport, and later mutable-resource protocols\\n-   remain open.\\n-4. **Execution module and tunnelling protocol.** Specify Run snapshot and live-delta\\n-   schemas, snapshot-revision handoff, direct and reverse-tunnel transports,\\n-   multiplexing, authorization, connection fencing, command idempotency and result\\n-   lookup, backpressure, and adapter conformance. Branch/session-scoped placement,\\n-   one current authority, epoch-targeted commands, executor-local raw mechanics,\\n-   fresh-snapshot reconnect recovery, executor-local journal versus portable\\n-   checkpoint separation, and rejection rather than relay queuing while offline\\n-   are settled. Also define how adapters advertise same-node process-recovery\\n-   guarantees without exposing provider-native journal schemas.\\n-5. **Workspace binding details.** Specify switching, archival, access-mode\\n-   changes, reauthorization, retention, availability detection and debounce, and\\n-   the exact recovery behavior for active versus not-yet-started Runs. Sticky\\n-   affinity and the four outage modes are settled.\\n-6. **Workspace registry privacy and liveness.** Define handle creation, path\\n-   redaction, labels, capability advertisement, online/offline state, stale\\n-   registrations, and what metadata each surface may see.\\n-7. **General permission model.** Extend `EnterWorkspace` into reusable scoped\\n-   `allow`/`ask`/`deny` rules, persistent approvals, revocation, audit, prompt\\n-   lifecycle, and separation among mount, filesystem, network, secret, device,\\n-   publish, and destructive-operation authority.\\n-8. **Sandbox-definition layering.** Decide which environment settings are global,\\n-   per user, per Agent Profile, per node, or per project; how candidate changes are\\n-   validated; and how accepted revisions, activation, rollback, and garbage\\n-   collection work.\\n-9. **Sandbox threat model and backend.** Define trusted versus hostile workloads,\\n-   network policy, CPU/memory limits, host interfaces, secret access, filesystem\\n-   isolation, and whether containers are sufficient or VM isolation is required.\\n-10. **Worktree lifecycle.** Define branch naming, base revision, concurrent Runs,\\n-   uncommitted source changes, publish/merge behavior, retention, cleanup, and\\n-   recovery after daemon failure.\\n-11. **Inference routing and broker protocol.** Define destination selection,\\n-    Workspace egress enforcement, context minimization, streaming, accounting,\\n-    provider failures, node-local credentials, and model continuity across\\n-    segments.\\n-12. **Knowledge synchronization and absorption.** Specify how each transport or\\n-    provider proves replica coverage, handles shallow or partial history, observes\\n-    and fences accepted refs, reports stale-base or divergent candidates, resumes\\n-    queued proposals, and gates pruning, deletion-record compaction, or physical\\n-    purge on verified completeness. Also specify provider capability discovery, normalized raw Git\\n-    and forge proposal lifecycles, revision authenticity, the absorption Skill,\\n-    provenance, and the optional UI advisory signal.\\n-13. **Shared and organization knowledge policy mechanics.** The directional modes\\n-    are selected: `online-only`, `offline-read`, and `offline-propose`, while\\n-    `full-replica` is initially personal-only. Later define authorization leases,\\n-    retained-copy handling, revalidation, candidate acceptance, revocation,\\n-    migration, and cross-boundary information flow.\\n-14. **CLI contract.** Define new versus resumed Chats, cwd-only advertisement,\\n-    daemon discovery, shared local store, local provider configuration, link and\\n-    unlink behavior, and degraded operation.\\n-15. **Android contract.** Define offline model support, Android assistant\\n-    integration, bounded device tools, local storage, remote Run steering,\\n-    handoff, waiting, and user-visible executor state.\\n-16. **Node Protocol schemas and storage decomposition.** Specify the shared core\\n-    envelope, per-module version and capability negotiation, authorization scopes,\\n-    compatibility policy, and conformance suites for `core.*`, `realm.*`,\\n-    `execution.*`, `sync.*`, and `admin.*`. Then decide which domain types belong in\\n-    shared TypeScript packages and which embedded store implements the personal-node\\n-    side. The modular contract and local-only-by-default administration boundary are\\n-    settled; the hub remains the multi-user service boundary and its current\\n-    implementation remains NestJS/Postgres.\\n-17. **Roadmap decomposition.** Split the direction into independently valuable\\n-    horizons. Personal Knowledge Spaces remain the nearer-term priority; full\\n-    node replication and distributed execution should not be disguised as one\\n-    deliverable.\\n-\\n-## 10. Recommended next discussion\\n-\\n-Continue with the **initial Personal Realm synchronization contract**. Enrollment\\n-now has a disposable-identity initial contract, explicit revocation limits, and\\n-separate key-recovery and multi-peer follow-ups. The resumable Chat/Run record\\n-scope and the transport mechanism are settled: every contact uses one\\n-reconciliation function over the immutable semantic `ChangeBatch` replication\\n-journal, selecting a consistent snapshot at a named frontier only when that\\n-compact prefix is needed.\\n-\\n-The next concrete question should be:\\n-\\n-> Must the first delivery require causal dependencies before atomically applying\\n-> a `ChangeBatch`, returning the missing dependency identities for sender\\n-> backfill, while deferring durable out-of-order staging until multi-peer or\\n-> multi-hop delivery creates a measured need for it?\\n-\\n-Do not choose Postgres versus SQLite, NixOS versus a Nix-built OCI image, or NestJS\\n-module boundaries before answering that question. Those are implementation\\n-choices downstream of the execution and replication contracts.\\n+## 9. Vision closure\\n+\\n+This product-direction exploration is closed for now. It is not blocked on\\n+protocol completeness. The discussion has established the product boundary,\\n+surface and node model, Workspace and Sandbox distinction, authority and failure\\n+semantics, storage-class boundaries, federation direction, first-delivery\\n+simplifications, north-star invariants, and staged shipping model.\\n+\\n+### 9.1 Closure rule\\n+\\n+Reopen the vision only when new evidence invalidates a settled product invariant\\n+or a prioritized delivery cannot fit the model. Exact schemas, APIs, state\\n+machines, storage engines, cryptographic mechanisms, retry algorithms, and\\n+conformance cases are capability-design work. They belong in focused artifacts\\n+when that capability is selected for delivery, not in an ever-growing list of\\n+questions that must be answered before the vision can finish.\\n+\\n+“Simple first” retains four constraints: preserve stable identity and provenance,\\n+keep authority and failure semantics explicit, record north-star follow-ups, and\\n+promote a follow-up only when a shipped topology or measured limitation requires\\n+it. It does not require predesigning every follow-up.\\n+\\n+### 9.2 Deferred specification backlog\\n+\\n+The remaining work is intentionally grouped by capability and does not block\\n+vision closure:\\n+\\n+1. **Personal Realm synchronization:** exact schemas, frontiers, retention,\\n+   authorship, fork representation, later mutable resources, deletion, and\\n+   artifact transfer.\\n+2. **Node enrollment and execution:** credentials, revocation delivery, Node\\n+   Protocol schemas, tunnelling, recovery guarantees, handoff state machines, and\\n+   conformance tests.\\n+3. **Workspace execution:** registry privacy, binding lifecycle, permissions,\\n+   Sandbox layering and isolation, and Git worktree lifecycle.\\n+4. **Knowledge and foreign authorities:** publication adapters, absorption,\\n+   coverage proof, shared replication policy, revocation, and cross-boundary\\n+   information flow.\\n+5. **Surfaces and inference:** CLI and Android contracts, provider routing,\\n+   credential brokering, model continuity, and degraded operation.\\n+6. **Delivery planning:** decompose only the next chosen product slice into an\\n+   OpenSpec change and actionable roadmap work. Personal Knowledge Spaces remain\\n+   the nearer-term priority; full Realm replication and distributed execution are\\n+   not one deliverable.\\n+\\n+New implementation questions go into the relevant focused artifact. They should\\n+not grow this research note or reopen the entire product direction by default.\\n+\\n+## 10. Recommended next action\\n+\\n+Stop hardening the federation protocol in the abstract. In a separate decision,\\n+choose whether to distill the stable product direction into `VISION.md` now or\\n+leave these notes as provenance until the next concrete product slice is chosen.\\n+Do not start another chain of low-level synchronization questions here.\\n \\n ## 11. Promotion boundary\\n*** End Patch\";\ntext(await tools.apply_patch(patch));\n",
